<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Baldwin II, Margrave of Flanders</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 865–<a href="/years/918">918</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> · Also known as Baldwin II of Flanders, Baldwin the Bald</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Baldwin II, called the Bald, was count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a> from 879 to 918, son of Baldwin I Iron Arm and Judith, daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-bald">Charles the Bald</a>. Using the disorder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> raids, he built fortresses and gathered counties, abbeys and royal lands into a single principality. He married Ælfthryth, daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfred-the-great">Alfred the Great</a>, and divided his lands between his sons Arnulf I and Adalulf.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Baldwin II was born around 865 to Margrave Baldwin I of Flanders and Judith, daughter of Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-bald">Charles the Bald</a>. He was nicknamed the Bald after his maternal grandfather. He ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a> from 879 to 918, and the early years of his rule were marked by a series of devastating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> raids.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>By 883 Baldwin was forced to move north to the Pagus Flandransis, which became the territory most closely associated with the Counts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a>. He built a series of wooden fortifications at Saint-Omer, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bruges">Bruges</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghent">Ghent</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kortrijk">Kortrijk</a>, and seized lands abandoned by royal and ecclesiastical officials. Many of these citadels later formed castellanies housing government, militia and local courts.</p><p>In 888 the Western Frankish king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-fat">Charles the Fat</a> was deposed, and several candidates sought the crown. As a grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-bald">Charles the Bald</a>, Baldwin could have competed, but he and others tried instead to persuade the East Frankish king Arnulf to take the West Frankish crown, which Arnulf declined. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odo-of-france">Odo, Count of Paris</a>, of the Robertine family, was made king. Relations between Odo and Baldwin worsened when Odo failed to support Baldwin's attempt to control the Abbey of Saint Bertin.</p><p>Odo attacked Baldwin at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bruges">Bruges</a> but could not prevail, and Baldwin went on to gain Artois, including the Abbey of St. Vaast. When that abbey came under Archbishop Fulk of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reims">Reims</a> in 900, Baldwin had the archbishop assassinated and was excommunicated by Pope Benedict IV. When Herbert I, Count of Vermandois, opposed his advance into the upper Somme valley, Baldwin had him assassinated too.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Between 893 and 899 Baldwin married Ælfthryth, daughter of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfred-the-great">Alfred the Great</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wessex">Wessex</a>. The aim of the Anglo-Flemish alliance was to help him control the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canche">Canche</a> valley. Baldwin died on 10 September 918. Some of his warriors wished to bury him beside his father at the Abbey of Saint Bertin, but that abbey did not admit women even after death and Ælfthryth wanted to lie beside him, so he was buried at Saint Peter's Abbey in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghent">Ghent</a>. This began a tradition lasting five generations that made Ghent the burial place of the early counts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a>. His eldest son Arnulf I of Flanders succeeded him, and his younger son Adalulf became the first Count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boulogne-sur-mer">Boulogne</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Baldwin II of Flanders” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His son <b>Baldwin II</b> (879–918), who married Ælfthryth, daughter of Alfred the Great, used the disorder of the Viking age to build fortresses and gather counties, abbeys and royal lands into a single principality.</blockquote><a href="/empire/county-of-flanders">County of Flanders · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Boulogne took shape in the late 9th and early 10th centuries under counts linked to the house of Flanders; Adalulf, a son of <b>Baldwin II of Flanders</b>, held it in the 910s.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-boulogne">County of Boulogne</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghent">Ghent</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/belgium">Belgium</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.05,3.73&amp;year=899" aria-label="Ghent on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Baldwin II, Margrave of Flanders"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5200 5756h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5200 5756h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=51.05,3.73&amp;year=899">What was here in 899 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
